Declare it as a required signal input and read it through the signal accessor — not as a decorator.

```ts
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy, input, computed } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-order-card',
  standalone: true,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `
    <h2>Order #{{ order().id }}</h2>
    <p>Total: {{ total() }}</p>
  `,
})
export class OrderCardComponent {
  readonly order = input.required<Order>();

  readonly total = computed(() => formatTotal(this.order()));
}
```

Key points:

- **`input.required<Order>()`** declares the input and its type in one place. It's read as a function call, `order()`, both in the template and in class code. Since the component requires the value, use `input.required` rather than `input(...)` with a default — an optional input would force you to handle `undefined` at every read site for no reason.
- **Explicit type argument** `input.required<Order>()`. Don't rely on inference from a default you don't have; state the type.
- **`OnPush`** is safe with signal inputs: reading `order()` in the template registers the dependency, so the view updates when the input changes without any `Default` fallback.
- **Derive with `computed()`**, don't mutate or copy the input into a separate signal. `computed(() => formatTotal(this.order()))` recomputes when the input changes and stays pure (no side effects). If you need to react with a side effect, that's `effect()`, not `computed()`.
- **If a child needs to change the value back up**, that's `output()` for an event, or `model()` if you want two-way binding — not a mutable field.
- **Constructor/field use is fine**: because `input()` is a signal, calling `order()` inside an `effect()` or `computed()` tracks it correctly; just don't call it before field initialization in the constructor body if you need the initial value there — set up the `effect()` as a field initializer instead.

If instead the input is genuinely optional (the parent may omit it), then `input<Order>()` returns `Order | undefined`, and you branch on `order()` (or `@if (order(); as o)`) rather than assuming presence. Match whichever contract the caller actually has.

Declare it with the signal-based `input()` function, not an `@Input()` decorator, since the rest of the codebase is signal-based. Use `input.required<Order>()` when the parent is guaranteed to pass it, or `input<Order | undefined>()` (or `input<Order>()` with a default) when the value is genuinely optional.

```ts
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy, input, computed } from '@angular/core';
import { Order } from './order.model';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-order-summary',
  standalone: true,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
  template: `
    @if (order(); as o) {
      <h2>Order {{ o.id }}</h2>
      <p>Total: {{ total() }}</p>
    }
  `,
})
export class OrderSummaryComponent {
  // required: parent must bind a value
  readonly order = input.required<Order>();

  // derive values off the input with computed(), don't recompute in the template
  readonly total = computed(() =>
    this.order().lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.price * line.qty, 0),
  );
}
```

Key points:

- **Read it as a signal**: `order()` in both the template and the class. Templates read signals directly, so `{{ order().id }}` is correct — no getter needed, and `OnPush` change detection registers the dependency automatically.
- **`input.required<Order>()`** produces `InputSignal<Order>`, so `order()` is typed `Order`, not `Order | undefined`. If the parent might not bind it, use `input<Order>()` and handle the `undefined` case (the `@if (order(); as o)` narrowing above shows one way), or `input(defaultOrder)` if a sensible default exists.
- **`input()` is read-only.** If the parent needs two-way binding, use `model()` instead and read it the same way (`this.order()`), assigning to it to propagate changes up.
- **Don't mutate the input signal.** `order.set(...)` is not available on an `InputSignal`; derive via `computed()` or convert to a local `signal()` if the component needs editable state (e.g. `linkedSignal` if you want it to reset when the input changes).
- **Keep `computed()` pure.** No HTTP calls or `signal.set()` inside `total()` — just derived value math, per the patterns rule.
- **Skip the `@Input()` decorator entirely.** Mixing decorator inputs and signal inputs in the same component is the ceremony you're trying to avoid, and decorator inputs don't participate in the signal-based `OnPush` dependency tracking the rest of the codebase relies on.

So the short answer: `readonly order = input.required<Order>();` and read it everywhere as `order()`.

